\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Selection function of the searched sample against a volume-limited reference census, both selected on the same parallax quality: Gaia\nobreakspace  {}DR3 sources with $\varpi >0$, $\sigma _\varpi /\varpi \le 0.1$ and $1/\varpi \le 40$\,pc (17\,566 objects, 8594 of them carrying a \texttt  {teff\_gspphot} estimate), against the 89 stars with at least one searched window. The searched stars are measured far better than the cut requires --- the worst fractional parallax error among them is 2.5 per cent --- so the two columns are selected alike, and loosening the cut to 0.2 adds no census object at all. 86 of the 89 searched stars are classified: 53 from the Gaia temperature, 25 from the SIMBAD spectral type where that temperature is absent, and 8 identified as white dwarfs; 3 carry no classification in either source. The SIMBAD route matters because \texttt  {teff\_gspphot} is missing preferentially for cool stars, which is the population the M\nobreakspace  {}dwarf statement is about. ``Ratio'' is the sample column fraction over the census column fraction; $>1$ is over-representation. \emph  {The strongest selection effect here is distance, not spectral type.}}}{4}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:selfunc}{{1}{4}{Selection function of the searched sample against a volume-limited reference census, both selected on the same parallax quality: Gaia~DR3 sources with $\varpi >0$, $\sigma _\varpi /\varpi \le 0.1$ and $1/\varpi \le 40$\,pc (17\,566 objects, 8594 of them carrying a \texttt {teff\_gspphot} estimate), against the 89 stars with at least one searched window. The searched stars are measured far better than the cut requires --- the worst fractional parallax error among them is 2.5 per cent --- so the two columns are selected alike, and loosening the cut to 0.2 adds no census object at all. 86 of the 89 searched stars are classified: 53 from the Gaia temperature, 25 from the SIMBAD spectral type where that temperature is absent, and 8 identified as white dwarfs; 3 carry no classification in either source. The SIMBAD route matters because \texttt {teff\_gspphot} is missing preferentially for cool stars, which is the population the M~dwarf statement is about. ``Ratio'' is the sample column fraction over the census column fraction; $>1$ is over-representation. \emph {The strongest selection effect here is distance, not spectral type.}}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The selection funnel, from the catalogued population within 40\,pc to the stars with a searched window. The three quantities the text keeps apart are the 168-entry work list the archive query returns, the 115{} stars a public field genuinely contains, and the 89{} that yielded a searchable window. The chain applied to what comes out of the funnel is Fig.\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:chain}.}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:funnel}{{2}{4}{The selection funnel, from the catalogued population within 40\,pc to the stars with a searched window. The three quantities the text keeps apart are the \NCensus -entry work list the archive query returns, the \NGenuinelyCovered {} stars a public field genuinely contains, and the \NStars {} that yielded a searchable window. The chain applied to what comes out of the funnel is Fig.~\ref {fig:chain}}{figure.2}{}}
