\citation{Mason2025}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces What the drift search reaches, as a physical selection function. Each point is the drift ceiling of one of the 402{} Class\nobreakspace  {}A windows, converted to line-of-sight acceleration, $|a|=c\,\dot  \nu /\nu $, against observing frequency; the shaded region above is unsearched. The boundary is flat because the grid was built at constant acceleration; in the units the search works in it is not, running 1.4\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at 115\,GHz to 10.5\,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at 872\,GHz. The ceiling takes two values, 3.60\,m\,s$^{-2}$ on 397{} windows and 4.00\,m\,s$^{-2}$ on the 5{} TRAPPIST--1{} ones, and it is each window's own ceiling that decides whether a given orbit is contained. The horizontal lines are the two most demanding known planets of any target, TRAPPIST--1 b{} (4.00\,m\,s$^{-2}$) and GJ 581 e{} (2.27\,m\,s$^{-2}$). Everything else of interest lies below the foot of the axis, including 30{} of the 39{} known planets in the sample and the two accelerations a transmitter on Earth would carry whether or not anyone intended it: the planet's rotation, 0.034\,m\,s$^{-2}$ at the equator, a factor 106{} below the ceiling, and its orbit, 0.0059\,m\,s$^{-2}$, a factor 607. TRAPPIST--1 b{} is the one planet above the default ceiling; it transits, so the edge-on case is the relevant one, and it lies above that ceiling for 29{} per cent of its orbital phase and above the widened one for 1{} per cent.}}{7}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:accel}{{4}{7}{What the drift search reaches, as a physical selection function. Each point is the drift ceiling of one of the \NWinA {} Class~A windows, converted to line-of-sight acceleration, $|a|=c\,\dot \nu /\nu $, against observing frequency; the shaded region above is unsearched. The boundary is flat because the grid was built at constant acceleration; in the units the search works in it is not, running \AccelDriftLo \,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqLo \,GHz to \AccelDriftHi \,kHz\,s$^{-1}$ at \AccelFreqHi \,GHz. The ceiling takes two values, \AccelCeilMed \,m\,s$^{-2}$ on \AccelNDefault {} windows and \AccelCeilMax \,m\,s$^{-2}$ on the \AccelNWide {} \AccelWideStar {} ones, and it is each window's own ceiling that decides whether a given orbit is contained. The horizontal lines are the two most demanding known planets of any target, \AccelWorstName {} (\AccelWorstVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$) and \AccelSecondName {} (\AccelSecondVal \,m\,s$^{-2}$). Everything else of interest lies below the foot of the axis, including \AccelNPlBelowAxis {} of the \AccelNPlanets {} known planets in the sample and the two accelerations a transmitter on Earth would carry whether or not anyone intended it: the planet's rotation, \AccelEarthRot \,m\,s$^{-2}$ at the equator, a factor \AccelRotMargin {} below the ceiling, and its orbit, \AccelEarthOrb \,m\,s$^{-2}$, a factor \AccelOrbMargin . \AccelWorstName {} is the one planet above the default ceiling; it transits, so the edge-on case is the relevant one, and it lies above that ceiling for \TrapPhaseHi {} per cent of its orbital phase and above the widened one for \TrapPhaseLo {} per cent}{figure.4}{}}
