\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The chain that disposes of a threshold crossing, with the number surviving each step: 56{} crossings, 19{} identified as carbon monoxide by the stellar-frame line mask, 37{} unattributed, 1{} outranking every one of the 512{} control positions, and none recurring in a later epoch. Every count is read from the ledger, so the figure and Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:ledger} cannot disagree. The visibility fit sits outside the column, because it is evaluated at the position and in the cell where the search already found an excess (\S  \ref  {sec:vistest}).}}{12}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:chain}{{7}{12}{The chain that disposes of a threshold crossing, with the number surviving each step: \NCross {} crossings, \NAttributed {} identified as carbon monoxide by the stellar-frame line mask, \NUnattributed {} unattributed, \NUnattrRankFlagged {} outranking every one of the \NCtrl {} control positions, and none recurring in a later epoch. Every count is read from the ledger, so the figure and Table~\ref {tab:ledger} cannot disagree. The visibility fit sits outside the column, because it is evaluated at the position and in the cell where the search already found an excess (\S \ref {sec:vistest})}{figure.7}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The crossing ledger.} Every one of the 56{} threshold crossings: the star, its execution block, the ALMA band, the crossing frequency as the correlator delivered it, the same frequency in the star's rest frame, the search statistic $T_\star $, whether the star outranks all 512{} control positions, the nearest masked transition, the stellar-frame offset $\Delta v_\star $ from it, and the disposition. A crossing is attributed where $|\Delta v_\star |\le 50$\,km\,s$^{-1}$; the transition named is the nearest one in that frame, so for a crossing far from every transition it is the magnitude of $\Delta v_\star $ and not the name that carries the information. $\nu _\star $ follows from $\nu _{\rm  topo}$ by the barycentric term of that block's own epoch and the star's systemic velocity, and nothing else, so the last column is reproducible from the two before it. Block identifiers omit the constant \texttt  {A002\_} prefix.}}{13}{table.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ledger}{{7}{13}{\textbf {The crossing ledger.} Every one of the \NCross {} threshold crossings: the star, its execution block, the ALMA band, the crossing frequency as the correlator delivered it, the same frequency in the star's rest frame, the search statistic $T_\star $, whether the star outranks all \NCtrl {} control positions, the nearest masked transition, the stellar-frame offset $\Delta v_\star $ from it, and the disposition. A crossing is attributed where $|\Delta v_\star |\le \MaskHalfKms $\,km\,s$^{-1}$; the transition named is the nearest one in that frame, so for a crossing far from every transition it is the magnitude of $\Delta v_\star $ and not the name that carries the information. $\nu _\star $ follows from $\nu _{\rm topo}$ by the barycentric term of that block's own epoch and the star's systemic velocity, and nothing else, so the last column is reproducible from the two before it. Block identifiers omit the constant \texttt {A002\_} prefix}{table.7}{}}
